WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.290
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.470
Some time ago, smartphone cameras were trying
to outpace each other by adding more and more

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.099
megapixels to their specification sheet.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:18.730
The difference between a half megapixel image
and a 4 megapixel image was night and day.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.570
However, nowadays, we have entered into diminishing
returns as most newer mobile cameras support

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.999
8 or more megapixels.

00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:30.250
At this point, a further resolution increase
doesn't lead to significantly more convincing

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.061
photos - and here is where the processing
software takes the spotlight.

00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:40.580
This paper is about an AI-based technique
that takes a poor quality photo and automatically

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:41.880
enhances it.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.900
Here, you can already see what a difference
software can make to these photos.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.010
Many of these photos were taken with an 8-year-old
mobile camera and were enhanced by the AI.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.240
This is insanity.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.900
Now, before anyone thinks that by enhancement,
I am referring to the classic workflow of

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.080
adjusting white balance, color levels and
hues.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.379
No, no, no.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:10.409
By enhancement, I mean the big, heavy hitters
like recreating lost details via super resolution

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:17.399
and image inpainting, image deblurring, denoising,
and recovering colors that were not even recorded

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:18.450
by the camera.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.789
The idea is the following: first, we shoot
a lot of photos from the same viewpoint with

00:01:23.789 --> 00:01:30.939
a bunch of cameras, ranging from a relatively
dated iPhone 3GS, other mid-tier mobile cameras,

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:33.240
and a state of the art DSLR camera.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.240
Then, we hand over this huge bunch of data
to a neural network that learns the typical

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.889
features that are preserved by the better
cameras and lost by the worse ones.

00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:48.090
The network does the same with relating the
noise patterns and color profiles to each

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.090
other.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.220
And then, we use this network to recover these
lost features and pump up the quality of our

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.679
lower tier camera to be as close as possible
to a much more expensive model.

00:01:59.679 --> 00:02:01.899
Super smart idea, loving it.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:03.950
And you know what is even more brilliant?

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.380
The validation of this work can take place
in a scientific manner, because we don't need

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.290
to take a group of photographers who will
twirl their moustaches and judge these photos.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:17.040
Though, I'll note that this was also done
for good measure.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:23.411
But, since we have the photos from the high-quality
DSLR camera, we can take the bad photos, enhance

00:02:23.411 --> 00:02:28.770
them with the AI, and compare this output
to the real DSLR's output.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.020
Absolutely brilliant.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.060
The source code and pre-trained networks and
an online demo is also available.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:40.050
So, let the experiments begin, and make sure
to leave a comment with your findings!

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.480
What do you think about the outputs shown
in the website?

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.470
Did you try your own photo?

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:45.830
Let me know in the comments section.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:51.280
A high-quality validation section, lots of
results, candid discussion of the limitations

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.800
in the paper, published source code, pretrained
networks and online demos that everyone can

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.930
try free of charge.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.520
Scientists at ETH Zürich maxed this paper
out.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.110
This is as good as it gets.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.590
